<article class="env-proposition" id="06KS">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06KS">Proposition <span data-tag="06KS">9.4.7.20</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an essentially small $\infty $-category. Then $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is accessible if and only if it is idempotent-complete. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof of Proposition <a data-tag="06KS" href="/tag/06KS">9.4.7.20</a>.</strong>
       Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an essentially small $\infty $-category which is idempotent-complete; we wish to show that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is accessible (the reverse implication follows from Remark <a data-tag="06KK" href="/tag/06KK">9.4.7.14</a>). Let $\kappa $ be a small uncountable regular cardinal for which $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is locally $\kappa $-small, and let $\lambda &gt; \kappa $ be a small regular cardinal for which $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is essentially $\lambda $-small. Applying Proposition <a data-tag="06KV" href="/tag/06KV">9.4.7.24</a>, we deduce that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ can be identified with an $\operatorname{Ind}_{\lambda }$-completion of itself. In particular, $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $\lambda $-accessible. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
